  "prompt_text": "The figure is a compact map of nonlinear optical processes used for quantum light sources, organized by the order of the material nonlinearity, with second-order effects on top and third-order effects below. It compares how different input photons combine, split, or mix to produce new frequencies, using vertical energy-level-style arrows and simple frequency relations.\n\nIn the χ(2) section, the main themes are frequency conversion and parametric processes: second-harmonic and sum-frequency generation create higher-frequency output, while parametric down-conversion and difference-frequency generation produce lower-frequency outputs or split one input into two. A related branch highlights parametric amplification and frequency conversion driven by a pump.\n\nIn the χ(3) section, the figure shows third-harmonic generation and several forms of four-wave mixing. These include nondegenerate and degenerate four-wave mixing, which generate signal and idler fields and are associated with parametric gain or comb generation, as well as phase-conjugation and Bragg-scattering four-wave mixing, which are presented as frequency-conversion mechanisms.\n\nOverall, the image summarizes the main nonlinear pathways for generating, amplifying, and converting optical frequencies in quantum photonics.",
